i've announced five new measures  we're protecting jobs through the new  jobs  support scheme the government will  directly support  the wages of people in work giving  businesses who face depressed demand  through the difficult winter months the  option of keeping employees  in a job on shorter hours rather  than making them redundant we're also  extending the self-employed scheme  to sit alongside that and i've announced  a range of measures  to ease cash flow concerns for  businesses  allowing them to focus on protecting  jobs  pay as you grow will ease the repayment  terms  for bounce-back loans we're giving  people more time to pay back their  deferred taxes  and i'm cancelling the planned increase  in vat  for hospitality and tourism through to  the end  of march i know people are anxious and  afraid right now  but i want them to know that we have a  plan to protect jobs  and support businesses and what was true  at the beginning of this crisis  remains true it's on all of us  to get through this together  let's take a look at those measures  first announced in the commons this  afternoon  the jobs support scheme will begin in  november and run for six  months employees in smaller or  struggling businesses who are working  reduced hours will get a third of their  lost salary topped up by the government  the self-employment income support  scheme has also been extended  and there's a pay-as-you-grow extension  to the bounce-back loan scheme to give  businesses  up to 10 years to repay debts for the  hospitality and tourism sectors  the va t cut to 5 has been extended  until spring in a moment we'll be  hearing from kate mccann  in westminster first to our business  presenter ian king who's in the city  for us uh ian a big moment for the  chancellor today furlough coming to an  end  a new package to get us through the  winter what does it look like  well it's considerably less generous uh  than the existing furlough scheme is the  most obvious point to make sarah at the  moment uh  an employee is entitled to 80  of their wages for doing nothing now  they'll have to be working at least a  third of their hours and if they do so  they'll be guaranteed  probably according to the treasury some  77  of their wages now it's particularly  unfavorable to employers interestingly  enough because at the moment  uh the employer is making a  substantially lower contribution  to uh their employees salary some 20  well this rises to 55 under the new  scheme  which is worth pointing out it's  actually much much less generous  than the scheme in germany on which uh  mr sunak's new scheme has been based the  german scheme of course credited  with avoiding uh big job losses in the r  and the aftermath of the financial  crisis  a decade ago it's an idea that's been uh  picked up and uh run with by rishi sunak  but as i say  the uh government support scheme that's  been unveiled today is substantially  less generous  than uh the uh scheme in germany on  which it was based i find that very  interesting  and there are certainly concerns which  the chancellor did nothing  to try and run away from in his press  conference a moment ago that this is  still going to lead to a big rise  in unemployment now the chancellor  didn't say how big the rise in  unemployment would be  on the back of this he merely referred  people to the bank of england's forecast  which is around seven and a half percent  uh joblessness and the opi the office of  budget responsibility well under their  most stops in the optimistic scenario  unemployment rises to 9.7 so either way  you're probably  looking at a big rise in unemployment  it's difficult to say precisely how much  but looking at some of the uh forecasts  that  are starting to drop into my inbox from  uh economists around the city  we're looking at uh for example uh simon  french over at pamela gordon  he says that he was modeling a rise of  one and a half million  in the uh unemployment total between now  and the end of the year he says this  will probably bring that down by between  a quarter  and a third but either way you're still  looking at probably an increase  in unemployment of over a million well  that's undoubtedly the biggest measure  other measures include an extension of  the vat  temporary cut in vat for hospitality  businesses that will last until  the end of the financial year now there  are deferrals for other self-employed in  terms of  when they have to pay their  self-assessment tax bills and they've  been  extension to the various government loan  schemes which have already paid out  something like 58 billion pounds  in support to businesses up and down the  country that money of course  has to be repaid sometime and there's  been a lot of anxiety  among banks here in the city that  they're ever going to see that money  back because the  government guarantees only last so long  so those were the main measures there  was nothing  on business rates relief which is  something that i think will be of great  concern  to uh the retail sector in particular  but worth pointing out that big  set-piece moment sarah right at the  opening  when uh mr sunak was unveiling his uh  his speech  he was flanked by francis o'grady of the  tuc and by dane carolyn fairburne from  the cbi  very unusual to see those two in public  and uh  variously hinting that uh they were  supportive of this measure i mean  it was a quite an astute political move  to get both the tuc  and the cbi on side i feel for this and  accordingly  it's why you're uh you're seeing uh some  there you go you can see the pictures  now  then carolyn ferben there on the right  of the screen francis o'grady from the  tc on the left-hand side  of the screen as i say very very  interesting that they were involved in  the  presentation of that speech to get the  employers  and the unions on side is no small  matter  and it's very striking that not only has  the cbi been very favorable  of this but so the federation of small  businesses and the british  chambers of commerce because of course  it's their members ultimately who are  going to be making these  very very difficult decisions about uh  whether they make people unemployed or  not
 

